> I'm curious: How can that be detected?
> Shouldn't the cheat file be identical to the original one in every
> detectable way?

That is exactly why it works. Before restoring a saved game, I
calculate the cryptographic hash of the .sav file:

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptographic_hash_function

In this case, SHA-1. Then I look up the hash in a fixed-size list. If
it is not in the list it is added, purging the oldest hash if
necessary. If it is in the list, this exact saved game has been
restored before, so the cheating flag is raised and stored in the
saved game thereafter.

In order to force the .sav file hash to change even if the game state
hasn't, the number of times the current game has been saved, and the
number of seconds from a fixed date are stored in the .sav file.

There is a possibility of collisions (two different .sav files having
the same hash) but it is highly unlikely, especially when only the
last 10 hashes are remembered. Ideally, you could keep a copy of the
entire saved game somewhere for comparison, but that would take up too
much space. I keep the hashes in the Windows registry, taking up only
a few hundred bytes.

>
> Or.. do you check something in the logfile or record file against the save
